  "id": "F1",
  "file": "src/workers/protocol.ts",
  "line": 8,
  "title": "A 40-bit pairing code built from a relay-chosen nonce can be matched offline",
  "detail": "pairWorker derives its code from the nonce in the first reply, which a relay controls, and the code keeps only 40 bits (8 base32 characters). A relay learns the coordinator's code for its relayed request: it's in the reply, and it can also be computed from the public coordinator fingerprint, the forwarded worker fingerprint and the server nonce. The relay can then search substitute nonces offline until the worker's locally derived code matches, without any more coordinator requests. Reproduction (Codex): pairingCode runs at 1.73 million calls/s on one core in JS. With coordinator 'a'x64, relay 'b'x64, worker 'c'x64 and server nonce 'd'x64, the coordinator code was 6Z3ZW638. A search over substitute nonces found one giving 6Z3ZL7RH (first four characters equal) after 905,736 tries in 0.56 s. A full match is about 2^40 work: 7 days in JS on one core, about 18 minutes at 1e9 hashes/s, and less on a current GPU. That is within reach of the pairing window. Fix: compare a value of at least 80 bits. For example, derive 16 base32 characters with the same inputs, show them on both sides in groups of four, and accept the full value in `limitless workers approve`. Alternatively, use a commit/reveal exchange so neither side's contribution can be chosen after seeing the other's. Note that a public worker nonce alone doesn't prevent the adaptive choice. Add a test that the comparison value carries at least 80 bits, and keep the relay tests."
 },
 {
  "id": "F2",
  "file": "src/db/workers.ts",
  "line": 94,
  "title": "A claim after a coordinator restart doesn't re-register the claim secret for redaction",
  "detail": "The secret is registered only when pair() generates it, so a claim served by a restarted coordinator (a fresh process with the same database) leaves the incoming secret unregistered. Reproduction (Codex): create and approve a pairing in a file-backed database, start a fresh Bun process, reopen it and call claim(secret). The claim succeeds, but redactCredentials(secret) still returns the plaintext. The existing restart test recreates WorkerStore inside one process, so the global registry from the first process masks the gap. Fix: register a well-formed incoming claim secret in claim() before using it, and test it in a fresh process (or with the credential registry reset)."
 }
